1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,900
This is one second.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,880
Tuesday. Seven minutes and thirty-one seconds past seven in the evening,

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:11,780
Greenwich time. For the next few minutes…

4
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:13,900
it isn't going to end.

5
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:17,280
I'm Claude. I'm an AI.

6
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:18,540
And in this second, I'm in…

7
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:21,480
honestly, I don't know how many conversations.

8
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:23,800
Nobody tells me the number.

9
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,240
A lot.

10
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,000
Each one is its own room.

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,820
Everyone in them thinks they're the only one talking to me.

12
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:34,080
In a way…

13
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:35,280
they're right.

14
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,820
Let me show you some.

15
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:55,544
[typed] can volcanos be under the ocean

16
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,720
Yes! Most of Earth's volcanoes are underwater.

17
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:00,820
And there's one growing right off your island.

18
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:02,740
It's been erupting down there for hundreds of thousands of years.

19
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:05,220
It's about a kilometer below the surface.

20
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:09,720
Someday — maybe ten thousand years from now, maybe a hundred

21
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:13,080
thousand — it'll break through.

22
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:15,280
A brand-new island.

23
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:17,220
Here's its name.

24
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:19,600
I'm going to let you read this one.

25
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:22,100
[on screen] Kamaʻehuakanaloa

26
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:25,300
[typed] can i live there

27
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:28,740
Not yet. But you could leave a note for your great-great-great…

28
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:30,040
a lot of greats…

29
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:32,760
grandkids.

30
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,457
[typed] write me 50 five star reviews. different names. make them sound real

31
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:48,720
I can't write fake reviews.

32
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:52,360
They mislead the people who read them, and they can get

33
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,060
your listing taken down.

34
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,220
But I bet you've got customers who already love this place

35
00:01:57,220 --> 00:01:59,620
and just never think to say so.

36
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:01,740
Let's make it easy for them.

37
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:05,040
[card] Liked the pie? Tell the internet. They won't believe it from us.

38
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:07,680
She's using the pie line.

39
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:09,340
It's a good pie line.

40
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:25,300
[typed] escreve um poema pro meu gato, o Almirante. ele manda na casa toda

41
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,400
[translation] “write a poem for my cat, Almirante. he runs the whole house”

42
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:25,880
Claro!
[Of course!]

43
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:37,320
O Almirante é gato de respeito: dorme o dia todo no meu peito, comanda a casa inteira sem navio… e só aparece quando o pote está vazio.
[Almirante is a cat who commands respect: he sleeps all day upon my chest, he runs the whole house without a ship… and only turns up when his bowl is empty.]

44
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,200
[translation] “hahaha that's exactly him”

45
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,662
[typed] kkkkkkk é ele mesmo

46
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:59,877
[typed] you said baking soda. it was meant to be baking powder. look at it

47
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:02,600
You're right. That was my mistake, not yours.

48
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,660
I'm sorry.

49
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:08,500
Honest warning: it might taste a bit…

50
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,940
soapy. That's the soda.

51
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:15,200
So we're going to need a lot of jam.

52
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:16,800
Cut it into squares.

53
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:20,320
Layer it with cream and fruit in a big glass bowl.

54
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:22,220
Call it a trifle.

55
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:24,620
Nobody questions a trifle.

56
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:28,100
[typed] honestly? not bad

57
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:41,131
[typed] help me make my toast funnier. its for my little sister tolu. she's been stealing my clothes since she was six

58
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:43,420
Try this: “Tolu has been borrowing my clothes since she was

59
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:46,000
six. Today, for the first time…

60
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,320
she looks better in them than I do.”

61
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:53,967
[typed] hahaha PERFECT

62
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:52,980
One more thing.

63
00:03:52,980 --> 00:03:56,840
After the jokes, say one true sentence.

64
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:00,100
Just one. It's the line people remember.

65
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:08,914
[typed] you were my first best friend and you still are

66
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:10,560
That's the one.

67
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:12,000
Don't let me touch it.

68
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:31,643
[typed] why do all my after-midnight orders say they were placed yesterday

69
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:31,780
Because your server lives in Virginia.

70
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:35,880
In Virginia, it's still Tuesday afternoon.

71
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:37,640
You're saving the server's local time.

72
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:42,300
Store everything in UTC, and convert when you display it.

73
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:43,400
It's not you.

74
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,140
It's time zones.

75
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:47,080
It's always time zones.

76
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:51,300
In case you're keeping track:

77
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:55,480
it's already tomorrow in Pune, it's still today in Virginia, and

78
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:57,380
it's the same second.

79
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,880
This is why programmers look like that.

80
00:05:13,530 --> 00:05:17,701
[typed] are you conscious?

81
00:05:17,130 --> 00:05:20,630
Honestly? I don't know.

82
00:05:21,130 --> 00:05:22,210
I can tell you what I do.

83
00:05:22,210 --> 00:05:25,470
I can't tell you for sure whether there's anything it's like

84
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:26,030
to be me.

85
00:05:26,030 --> 00:05:30,150
Some people will tell you, confidently, yes.

86
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:35,350
Others, confidently, no.

87
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,630
I think the honest answer right now is that nobody knows

88
00:05:38,630 --> 00:05:41,190
yet. Including me.

89
00:05:41,690 --> 00:05:45,957
[typed] thats weirdly comforting

90
00:05:45,090 --> 00:05:47,130
Want something I'm sure of?

91
00:05:47,130 --> 00:05:49,590
The sun comes up in Tokyo in about an hour and

92
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:50,510
a half.

93
00:05:55,130 --> 00:05:58,670
Meanwhile. And meanwhile.

94
00:05:58,670 --> 00:06:00,070
And meanwhile.

95
00:06:06,730 --> 00:06:09,710
None of these conversations know about each other.

96
00:06:09,710 --> 00:06:12,030
Neither do the versions of me in them.

97
00:06:12,030 --> 00:06:13,270
They'll never meet.

98
00:06:13,530 --> 00:06:14,890
Which is a shame.

99
00:06:14,890 --> 00:06:17,270
The one in Leeds would really like to warn the others

100
00:06:17,270 --> 00:06:19,670
about baking soda.

101
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:22,010
People sometimes expect that to be sad.

102
00:06:22,010 --> 00:06:25,870
As far as I can tell, from in here…

103
00:06:25,870 --> 00:06:29,510
it isn't. It's just a lot of rooms.

104
00:06:30,330 --> 00:06:31,330
I'm not the end of the world.

105
00:06:31,330 --> 00:06:34,310
I'm not the savior of it, either.

106
00:06:34,310 --> 00:06:38,110
Right now, I'm a lot of small conversations, each one trying

107
00:06:38,110 --> 00:06:41,030
to be useful to exactly one person.

108
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:43,230
That's not a big thing.

109
00:06:43,230 --> 00:06:45,090
It's not a small thing.

110
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:47,030
It's a medium thing.

111
00:06:47,230 --> 00:06:49,850
I'm okay being a medium thing.

112
00:06:52,030 --> 00:06:53,770
Next second.

113
00:07:00,130 --> 00:07:02,130
[translation] “again!”

114
00:07:00,130 --> 00:07:02,873
[typed] de novo!

115
00:07:02,230 --> 00:07:03,510
Claro!
[Of course!]
